course: dictionary
lesson: 12825
title: Emit: sending it out
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To emit something means to send it out. A lamp can emit light, a fire can emit heat, and a horn can emit a loud noise. You can also emit a smell or a gas.
explain: Look at the words around emit to check its meaning. «The lamp emits a soft, yellow light into the dark room.» The light is coming out of the lamp, so emit means send out.
explain: Everyday speech often uses other words for emit, such as give off or send out. «The bakery gives off a warm smell of bread.» Both sentences mean the same kind of thing.
fact: Emit is a verb, and its forms are emit, emits, emitted and emitting.
fact: «The Sun emits light and heat.»
fact: «A light bulb emits light when electricity flows through it.»
fact: In science and climate lessons, cars are said to emit carbon dioxide, and countries try to emit less greenhouse gas.
fact: Give off and send out are everyday words that you can use instead of emit.
exercise: «The Sun emits light and heat.» What does emits mean here? | *Sends out light and heat | Stops light and heat | Hides light and heat | Takes in light and heat
exercise: Which phrase means the same as emit? | Cover up | Wrap tightly | *Send out | Pull in
exercise: «The bell ___ a loud ring yesterday.» Which form of emit fits the gap? | emit | emitting | *emitted | emits
test: «The old radio emitted a crackly sound.» What does emitted mean? | Wrapped the sound up | Hid the sound | *Sent out a sound | Stopped the sound
test: Countries try to emit less greenhouse gas. What does this ask them to do? | *Send out less of this gas | Dig deeper into the ground | Hear it louder | Buy more of this gas
exam: «The fire ___ smoke and heat into the room.» Which word fits the gap? | emitted | *emits | emitting | emit
